vscode: click between the last letter and the semicolon
open: total.ts

@brief
Ten times: pluralise `tax` so the line ends `+ order.taxes;`. The
semicolon is in the way, which is the entire reason `ge` exists.

@keys
| `$` | end of the line |
| `ge` | back to the end of the previous word |
| `a` | append after the cursor |

@hints
`$` lands you on the semicolon. One `ge` from there is the end of `tax`.
--
Three keys of movement, two of typing. If you are using more than that,
you are travelling forwards from the start of the line.

@solution
    $ ge  aes<Esc>  :wq<Enter>

Eleven keystrokes, and the shape generalises to every trailing-punctuation
edit you will ever make: `$ge` is "the last real character on this line",
as opposed to `$`, which is the last character of any kind.

Closing parens, commas on object properties, semicolons, a trailing
backtick. Same three keys every time.

@start total.ts
const total = order.subtotal + order.tax;

@expected total.ts
const total = order.subtotal + order.taxes;
